WEBVTT - Most Fascinating Stories - Episode 312: Edward VI: The Boy King, the Protestant Reformation, and the Book of Common Prayer

1
00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:05.600
[Rebecca] Welcome to Most Fascinating Stories.

2
00:00:05.600 --> 00:00:06.880
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

3
00:00:06.880 --> 00:00:16.320
[Rebecca] Today, our Kings and Queens of England series chronicles the remarkable child, sovereign whose six-year reign permanently transformed the soul of English religion.

4
00:00:16.320 --> 00:00:19.360
[Rebecca] The fiercely intelligent boy king, Edward V.

5
00:00:19.360 --> 00:00:19.920
[Rebecca] I.

6
00:00:19.920 --> 00:00:26.800
[Rebecca] Here is George with the extraordinary story of the young Tudor Reformer.

7
00:00:26.800 --> 00:00:30.640
[George] On October 12, 1537, the eve of St.

8
00:00:30.640 --> 00:00:37.120
[George] Edward the Confessor, bells rang across London, bonfires blazed in every public square, and T.

9
00:00:37.280 --> 00:00:38.960
[George] Diem hymns echoed through St.

10
00:00:38.960 --> 00:00:40.320
[George] Paul's Cathedral.

11
00:00:40.320 --> 00:00:44.800
[George] Jane Seymour had given birth to a living male heir, Prince Edward.

12
00:00:44.800 --> 00:00:52.480
[George] King Henry VIII wept tears of joy, believing God had finally blessed his dynasty with an undisputed prince.

13
00:00:52.480 --> 00:01:05.280
[George] Raised in the nursery of Hampton Court Palace under the meticulous care of Lady Margaret Bryan, young Edward was sheltered with neurotic cleanliness, his rooms scrubbed with vinegar and scented water twice daily.

14
00:01:05.280 --> 00:01:16.080
[George] Endowed with extraordinary intellectual gifts, Edward was educated by England's foremost humanist and reformist scholars, including Sir John Cheek and Roger Asham.

15
00:01:16.080 --> 00:01:26.880
[George] By age eight, the young prince was reading Cicero and Aesop in original Latin, writing fluent Greek translations, and mastering French, geometry, and scripture.

16
00:01:26.880 --> 00:01:45.680
[George] Unlike his father, who remained doctrinally conservative Catholic in his theology, young Edward embraced radical evangelical Protestantism with fierce, unyielding conviction, viewing himself as a new Josiah, the Old Testament boy king who purified the temple and purged idolatry from the land.

17
00:01:45.680 --> 00:01:50.800
[George] Young Prince Edward's education was a tour de force of Renaissance pedagogical rigor.

18
00:01:50.800 --> 00:01:59.440
[George] Under Cheek and Ashem, the young boy rose at dawn to study scripture in Greek and Hebrew, followed by classical rhetoric and Roman history.

19
00:01:59.640 --> 00:02:06.440
[George] He maintained a personal Greek diary and composed elaborate essays debating the morality of papal supremacy.

20
00:02:06.440 --> 00:02:10.040
[George] His daily household was managed with solemn decorum.

21
00:02:10.040 --> 00:02:25.000
[George] Courtiers dropped to one knee whenever they entered his presence, and the young prince developed a precocious, cold moral seriousness, once chiding his companion for playing dice and rebuking his uncle Thomas Seymour for offering him secret pocket money.

22
00:02:25.000 --> 00:02:31.080
[George] When Henry VIII died in January 1547, Edward was nine years old.

23
00:02:31.080 --> 00:02:38.440
[George] Henry's will had established a balanced regency council of sixteen executors to govern equally until Edward reached eighteen.

24
00:02:38.440 --> 00:02:45.320
[George] But Edward's ambitious maternal uncle, Edward Seymour, Earl of Hertford, moved with lightning speed.

25
00:02:45.320 --> 00:02:53.560
[George] Securing custody of the young king, Seymour seized total power, naming himself Duke of Somerset and Lord Protector of the Realm.

26
00:02:53.560 --> 00:03:05.640
[George] Crowned at Westminster Abbey on February 20, 1547, young Edward rode through London in a coat of silver tissue, being hailed by Archbishop Cranmer as England's new Josiah.

27
00:03:05.640 --> 00:03:16.120
[George] Somerset immediately sought to fulfill Henry Viii's dying dream, uniting England and Scotland by marrying young Edward to four-year-old Mary, Queen of Scots.

28
00:03:16.120 --> 00:03:23.800
[George] When the Scottish Regency refused, Somerset led a modern combined arms army across the border in September 1547.

29
00:03:23.800 --> 00:03:33.960
[George] At the Battle of Pinky Klug near Musselburg, English naval bombardment and cavalry charges annihilated the Scottish Pike Army, killing 10,000 Scots.

30
00:03:33.960 --> 00:03:37.960
[George] But Somerset's aggressive, rough wooing backfired completely.

31
00:03:37.960 --> 00:03:44.520
[George] The Scottish lords smuggled Little Mary to France to marry the Dauphin, permanently welding Scotland to France.

32
00:03:44.640 --> 00:03:53.040
[George] The Battle of Pinky Klug was notable for being one of the first battles in British history where integrated naval gunnery played a decisive role.

33
00:03:53.040 --> 00:04:04.160
[George] English warships in the Firth of Fourth fired heavy cannon directly into the right flank of the advancing Scottish army on the beach, creating panic that enabled Somerset's cavalry to charge.

34
00:04:04.160 --> 00:04:21.120
[George] Yet Somerset's garrisoning policy across the Scottish lowlands proved financially ruinous, costing over 500,000 pounds and leaving English forces exhausted, ultimately forcing an ignominious withdrawal when French regular troops landed in Leith to assist the Scots.

35
00:04:21.120 --> 00:04:28.080
[George] Back in England, Somerset and Archbishop Cranmer unleashed a sweeping, iconoclastic religious revolution.

36
00:04:28.080 --> 00:04:35.600
[George] While Henry VIII had broken with Rome politically, the everyday liturgy had remained largely traditional Latin Catholic.

37
00:04:35.600 --> 00:04:41.120
[George] Under Edward VI, the physical and spiritual landscape of England was stripped bare.

38
00:04:41.120 --> 00:04:47.120
[George] Royal injunctions ordered the destruction of all shrines, rude screens and statues of saints.

39
00:04:47.120 --> 00:04:52.480
[George] Whitewash was slapped over vibrant medieval wall paintings depicting the Last Judgment.

40
00:04:52.480 --> 00:05:05.040
[George] Stained glass windows showing biblical saints were smashed and replaced with plain clear glass, and stone altars were hauled out of churches and replaced with simple wooden communion tables set in the middle of the nave.

41
00:05:05.040 --> 00:05:16.400
[George] The Chantries Act of 1547 abolished thousands of chantry chapels where priests were paid to pray for souls in purgatory, with their lands and revenues confiscated by the Crown.

42
00:05:16.400 --> 00:05:27.360
[George] In 1549, Parliament passed the Act of Uniformity, introducing Thomas Cranmer's Book of Common Prayer, the first complete English language liturgy in history.

43
00:05:27.360 --> 00:05:31.000
[George] For Cranmer, Latin was an obstacle to true faith.

44
00:05:31.000 --> 00:05:39.240
[George] For the first time, ordinary English parishioners heard the majesty of Scripture, baptism, and marriage in their own poetic native tongue.

45
00:05:39.240 --> 00:05:57.400
[George] Thomas Cranmer's Book of Common Prayer of 1549, revised into an even more radically Protestant edition in 1552, is one of the supreme literary achievements in the English language, ranking alongside the King James Bible and Shakespeare in its enduring impact on English prose.

46
00:05:57.400 --> 00:06:16.200
[George] Its majestic phrasing, earth to earth, ashes to ashes, dust to dust, and in the midst of life we are in death, entered the national consciousness, shaping British Protestant spirituality, marriage rights, and funeral services for over four centuries across the global Anglican communion.

47
00:06:16.200 --> 00:06:26.120
[George] The rapid religious and economic changes sparked catastrophic unrest across the kingdom in the summer of 1549, remembered as the year of many rallies.

48
00:06:26.120 --> 00:06:41.160
[George] In Devon and Cornwall, devout Catholics revolted against the new English prayer book in the Prayer Book Rebellion, declaring they would not accept the new service because it was like a Christmas game, demanding the return of Latin Mass and the Six Articles.

49
00:06:41.160 --> 00:06:47.960
[George] Cornish rebels besieged Exeter before being brutally crushed by royal troops and Italian and German mercenaries.

50
00:06:47.960 --> 00:06:55.480
[George] Meanwhile, in Norfolk, a massive economic rebellion erupted against landlords enclosing common pastures for sheep farming.

51
00:06:55.480 --> 00:07:05.880
[George] Led by yeoman Tanner Robert Kett, 16,000 peasants established an organized encampment at Mousehold Heath overlooking Norwich, demanding land reform.

52
00:07:05.880 --> 00:07:13.560
[George] Somerset, sympathetic to the grievances of the poor and earning the moniker, the good Duke, hesitated to crush the rebels.

53
00:07:13.560 --> 00:07:17.520
[George] John Dudley, Earl of Warwick, stepped into the vacuum.

54
00:07:17.520 --> 00:07:25.680
[George] Marching into Norfolk with veteran troops, Dudley crushed Kett's rebellion at Dussendale, hanging Kett from the walls of Norwich Castle.

55
00:07:25.680 --> 00:07:28.480
[George] Somerset's authority was fatally shattered.

56
00:07:28.480 --> 00:07:35.120
[George] Robert Kett's rebellion on Mousehold Heath was an extraordinarily disciplined demonstration of peasant democracy.

57
00:07:35.120 --> 00:07:43.200
[George] The rebels established their own court beneath a giant oak dubbed the Tree of Reformation, where Kett and his council dispensed justice.

58
00:07:43.200 --> 00:07:49.520
[George] Held daily prayers using the new English Book of Common Prayer, and forbade drunkenness and plunder.

59
00:07:49.520 --> 00:08:01.120
[George] When the Royal Herald arrived offering pardons if they dispersed, Kett famously responded that kings grant pardons to wicked men, but they were honest men seeking only justice for the oppressed.

60
00:08:01.120 --> 00:08:07.360
[George] In October 1549, the Royal Council revolted against Somerset's autocratic rule.

61
00:08:07.360 --> 00:08:13.840
[George] Somerset fled to Windsor Castle with the twelve-year-old king, hoping to use Edward as a human shield.

62
00:08:13.840 --> 00:08:23.520
[George] Edward, shivering in the cold, famously complained in his private journal, Methinks I am in a prison, here be no galleries, nor no gardens.

63
00:08:23.520 --> 00:08:26.720
[George] Somerset was arrested and stripped of the protectorate.

64
00:08:26.720 --> 00:08:33.200
[George] Power passed to John Dudley, who made himself Duke of Northumberland and Lord President of the Council.

65
00:08:33.200 --> 00:08:38.240
[George] Unlike Somerset, Northumberland did not assume the title of Lord Protector.

66
00:08:38.240 --> 00:08:47.280
[George] Instead, he skillfully cultivated young Edward, granting the fourteen-year-old king increasing influence in council meetings and state policy.

67
00:08:47.280 --> 00:08:50.560
[George] Edward proved to be an exceptionally diligent monarch.

68
00:08:50.560 --> 00:08:59.920
[George] He kept a detailed personal chronicle, attended council debates, and drafted state papers on currency reform, national defence, and poor relief.

69
00:09:00.440 --> 00:09:11.640
[George] In January 1552, when Somerset was implicated in a plot to overthrow Northumberland, Edward showed the cold, unyielding Tudor Steel inherited from his father.

70
00:09:11.640 --> 00:09:19.160
[George] The fifteen-year-old king signed his own uncle's death warrant without hesitation, recording in his diary with chilling brevity.

71
00:09:19.160 --> 00:09:25.080
[George] The Duke of Somerset had his head cut off upon Tower Hill between eight and nine o'clock in the morning.

72
00:09:25.080 --> 00:09:35.080
[George] John Dudley, Duke of Northumberland, was a military and administrative genius who restored stability to England's finances after Somerset's reckless expenditures.

73
00:09:35.080 --> 00:09:46.120
[George] He revalued the debased coinage, paid off foreign debts in Antwerp, and negotiated a pragmatic peace with France, surrendering Boulogne for 400,000 crowns.

74
00:09:46.120 --> 00:09:49.000
[George] Under Northumberland's encouragement, Edward V.

75
00:09:49.000 --> 00:09:52.360
[George] I took an active role in expanding English commerce.

76
00:09:52.360 --> 00:10:05.960
[George] Chartering the company of merchant adventurers to new lands in 1553, which sent Richard Chancellor to Moscow to establish trade relations with Tsar Ivan the Terrible, opening English maritime commerce to Russia.

77
00:10:05.960 --> 00:10:13.240
[George] By late 1552, England seemed destined for decades of stable Protestant rule under an adult Edward V.

78
00:10:13.240 --> 00:10:13.800
[George] I.

79
00:10:13.800 --> 00:10:20.440
[George] But in February 1553, the 15-year-old king fell ill with a violent cough and fever.

80
00:10:20.440 --> 00:10:25.400
[George] Though he rallied briefly in the spring, by May his condition was catastrophic.

81
00:10:25.400 --> 00:10:32.600
[George] He was dying of consumption, pulmonary tuberculosis, complicated by a severe respiratory infection.

82
00:10:32.560 --> 00:10:44.640
[George] Emmaciated, coughing up black blood, and struggling to breathe, Edward realized that under Henry Vii's Third Succession Act, his Catholic half-sister Mary was next in line for the throne.

83
00:10:44.520 --> 00:10:49.520
[George] For the fanatically Protestant boy king, Mary's accession was unthinkable.

84
00:10:49.520 --> 00:10:56.880
[George] She would dismantle his Reformation, bring back the Pope, and plunge England back into what he viewed as papist darkness.

85
00:10:56.880 --> 00:11:02.960
[George] Propped up on his pillows, Edward penned with his own hand his historic devise for the succession.

86
00:11:02.960 --> 00:11:16.320
[George] Bypassing both Mary and Elizabeth as illegitimate, Edward bequeathed the crown to his Protestant cousin, seventeen-year-old Lady Jane Grey, a brilliant granddaughter of Henry Vii's sister Mary Tudor.

87
00:11:16.320 --> 00:11:25.360
[George] Northumberland quickly married Jane to his younger son, Lord Guildford Dudley, and bullied the realm's judges into signing the illegal letters patent.

88
00:11:25.360 --> 00:11:31.760
[George] Edward's device for the succession was a desperate attempt by a dying boy to safeguard his religious legacy.

89
00:11:31.760 --> 00:11:45.840
[George] As the cough worsened and fever wasted his frame, Edward spent hours debating the legal technicalities with Lord Chief Justice Edward Montagu, furiously ordering the judges to draw up the letter's patent under pain of high treason.

90
00:11:45.840 --> 00:12:00.080
[George] Edward saw it as his holy duty before God to prevent Mary from reversing the Reformation, convinced that saving the spiritual souls of millions of English subjects outweighed the strict hereditary succession established by his father's will.

91
00:12:00.080 --> 00:12:08.080
[George] In early July 1553, at Greenwich Palace, fifteen-year-old King Edward VI entered his final agony.

92
00:12:08.080 --> 00:12:14.960
[George] Courtiers gathered outside his bedchamber whispered that his fingernails had fallen off and his skin was turning purple.

93
00:12:14.960 --> 00:12:29.360
[George] On the evening of July 6, 1553, Edward whispered his final prayer Lord God, deliver me out of this miserable and wretched life, and take me among thy chosen, how be it not my will, but thy will be done.

94
00:12:29.360 --> 00:12:35.240
[George] O my Lord God, defend this realm from papistry and maintain thy true religion.

95
00:12:35.240 --> 00:12:41.000
[George] Moments later, he died in the arms of his gentleman of the Privy Chamber, Sir Henry Sidney.

96
00:12:41.000 --> 00:12:51.000
[George] Four days later, sixteen-year-old Lady Jane Grey was brought to the Tower of London and proclaimed Queen of England, weeping in horror at the crown placed upon her head.

97
00:12:51.000 --> 00:12:54.200
[George] But England's deep dynastic loyalty lay with Henry V.

98
00:12:54.200 --> 00:12:56.360
[George] I.I.I.'s rightful daughter Mary.

99
00:12:56.360 --> 00:13:07.960
[George] Within nine days, Mary rallied tens of thousands of armed gentry in East Anglia, marching into London in triumph while Northumberland was arrested and Jane Grey imprisoned in the Tower.

100
00:13:07.960 --> 00:13:08.840
[George] Edward V.

101
00:13:08.840 --> 00:13:10.280
[George] I was buried in Henry V.

102
00:13:10.280 --> 00:13:20.360
[George] I.I.'s Lady Chapel at Westminster Abbey under a simple communion table, his six-year reign having forged the permanent ideological foundation of the English Reformation.

103
00:13:20.360 --> 00:13:30.520
[George] Lady Jane Grey, the innocent seventeen-year-old scholar who spent nine tragic days as Queen of England, was a pawn in Northumberland's dynastic ambition.

104
00:13:30.520 --> 00:13:44.120
[George] When Mary arrived in London, hailed by roaring crowds celebrating the legitimate Tudor bloodline, Northumberland was arrested in Cambridge, where he threw his hat in the air shouting for Queen Mary in a pathetic bid for mercy.

105
00:13:44.120 --> 00:13:48.440
[George] Six months later, Jane Grey was beheaded on Tower Green.

106
00:13:48.440 --> 00:13:49.560
[George] Though Edward V.

107
00:13:49.560 --> 00:13:55.080
[George] I died at just fifteen, the religious transformation he had championed proved permanent.

108
00:13:55.080 --> 00:14:04.280
[George] The Protestant liturgy, royal supremacy, and English prayer book survived Mary's brief counterreformation to become the permanent soul of England.

109
00:14:04.920 --> 00:14:08.840
[Rebecca] Thank you for joining us for this episode of Most Fascinating Stories.

110
00:14:08.840 --> 00:14:24.560
[Rebecca] If you enjoyed this chapter of Edward V.I., The Boy King, The Protestant Reformation, and the Book of Common Prayer, subscribe to the show, share it with a fellow history lover, and tune in next Tuesday for our next journey into history's most extraordinary events.

111
00:14:24.560 --> 00:14:28.480
[Rebecca] Until next time, I'm Rebecca.

